window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-2645e597941a2d38"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"customization\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"codex\">自定义 Codex</h1> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取文档的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>自定义功能可以让 Codex 按照你和团队的工作方式运行。</p> <p>在 Codex 中，多种自定义机制会协同工作：</p> <ul> <li><strong>项目指导文件（<code>AGENTS.md</code>）</strong>：提供持久有效的指令。</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-7ea5a1f5cc6a25bc\">记忆</a></strong>：保存从以往工作中学到的有用背景信息。</li> <li><strong>Skills</strong>：封装可重复使用的工作流程和领域知识。</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a></strong>：连接外部工具和共享系统。</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">子代理</a></strong>：把工作分配给专门处理特定任务的子代理。</li> </ul> <p>这些机制彼此互补，而不是互相竞争。<code>AGENTS.md</code> 用于塑造行为，记忆会延续本地上下文，Skills 用来封装可重复执行的流程，而 <a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a> 则把 Codex 连接到本地工作区以外的系统。</p> <p><a id=\"agents-guidance\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"agents\">AGENTS 指导文件</h2> <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> 为 Codex 提供持久的项目指导。它会随代码仓库一起保存，并在代理开始工作前生效。文件应保持简洁。</p> <p>可以在其中写下希望 Codex 每次在某个代码仓库中都遵守的规则，例如：</p> <ul> <li>构建和测试命令</li> <li>代码审查要求</li> <li>代码仓库专用约定</li> <li>目录专用指引</li> </ul> <p>如果代理对代码库作出了错误假设，请在 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 中纠正，并要求代理更新该文件，让修正能在之后继续生效。这是一种持续反馈机制。</p> <p><strong>更新 <code>AGENTS.md</code>：</strong>先只写最重要的指令。把反复出现的审查意见整理成规则，放在最贴近其适用范围的目录中；当你纠正代理时，也可以让它同步更新 <code>AGENTS.md</code>。</p> <p><a id=\"when-to-update-agentsmd\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"agentsmd\">何时应更新 <code>AGENTS.md</code></h3> <ul> <li><strong>反复犯同一类错误：</strong>添加一条规则。</li> <li><strong>读取了过多内容：</strong>如果代理找到了正确文件，却读了太多文档，就补充目录路由指引，说明应优先查看哪些目录或文件。</li> <li><strong>反复收到相同的 PR 反馈：</strong>把这类反馈写成规则。</li> <li><strong>在 GitHub 中：</strong>在拉取请求评论中提及 <code>@codex</code> 并提出请求（例如 <code>@codex add this to AGENTS.md</code>），让 Codex 将其写入 <code>AGENTS.md</code>。</li> <li><strong>自动检测规则漂移：</strong>可以使用<a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">定期任务</a>定期检查指导是否存在缺口（例如每天检查一次），并建议应在 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 中补充哪些内容。</li> </ul> <p>将 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 与能强制执行规则的工具配合使用，例如 pre-commit 钩子、代码检查器和类型检查器；它们可以在你发现问题之前捕捉错误，避免只依赖记忆来防错。</p> <p>Codex 可以从多个位置加载指导文件：开发者个人 Codex 主目录中的全局文件，以及团队可以纳入版本控制的代码库专用文件。工作目录越接近的文件，优先级越高。</p> <p>全局文件适合设定 Codex 与你沟通的方式（例如审查风格、详细程度和默认偏好）；代码库文件则应聚焦于团队和代码库本身的规则。</p> <p><strong>文件结构</strong></p> <ul> <li><code>~/.codex/</code></li> <li><code>AGENTS.md</code> — 全局文件（面向开发者个人）</li> <li><code>repo-root/</code></li> <li><code>AGENTS.md</code> — 代码库专用文件（面向团队）</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">使用 AGENTS.md 提供自定义指令</a></p> <h2 id=\"skills\">Skills</h2> <p>Skills 为可重复执行的工作流程提供可复用的能力。</p> <p>对于可重复使用的工作流程，Skills 往往是最合适的方式：它能包含更丰富的指令、脚本、参考资料和资源，同时仍可按需复用。</p> <p>Skill 文件夹可以在本地创建和迭代。Skills 会向代理提供可见信息（至少包括其元数据），因此 Codex 能够自行发现并选择合适的 Skill。这样一来，丰富的工作流程可以随时调用，而不必一开始就全部塞进上下文。</p> <p>如果某个工作流程已经有对应的插件，可以先安装插件，复用已经验证过的配置。当你要在团队间分发自己的工作流程，或把它与连接器打包在一起时，可以把它封装为 <a href=\"#/article/a-0d81218eacd2463c\">插件</a>。Skills 仍是创作格式；插件是可安装的分发单元。</p> <p>一个 Skill 通常包含 <code>SKILL.md</code> 文件，以及可选的脚本、参考资料和资源。</p> <p><strong>文件结构</strong></p> <ul> <li><code>my-skill/</code></li> <li><code>SKILL.md</code> — 必需：指令和元数据</li> <li><code>scripts/</code> — 可选：可执行脚本</li> <li><code>references/</code> — 可选：参考资料</li> <li><code>assets/</code> — 可选：模板</li> </ul> <p>Skill 目录可以包含 <code>scripts/</code> 文件夹，供 Codex 在执行工作流程时运行脚本（例如初始化数据或使用辅助工具）。当工作流程需要外部系统（例如任务跟踪器、浏览器或文档服务）时，可以与 <a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a> 搭配使用。</p> <p>示例 <code>SKILL.md</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-md\">---\nname: commit\ndescription: Stage and commit changes in semantic groups. Use when the user wants to commit, organize commits, or clean up a branch before pushing.\n---\n\n1. Do not run `git add .`. Stage files in logical groups by purpose.\n2. Group into separate commits: feat → test → docs → refactor → chore.\n3. Write concise commit messages that match the change scope.\n4. Keep each commit focused and reviewable.\n</code></pre> <p>适合使用 Skills 的场景：</p> <ul> <li>可重复的工作流程（例如发布前检查、审查流程或文档更新）</li> <li>团队专用知识</li> <li>需要示例的流程</li> </ul> <p>Skills 可以放在全局位置（供个人使用），也可以放在代码库专用位置（适用于团队项目，并纳入版本控制）。如果工作流程只适用于某个代码库，应将 Skill 放入 <code>.agents/skills</code> 目录；请将代码库专用 Skills 提交到 <code>.agents/skills</code>，而要在所有代码库中使用的 Skills 则放在个人目录。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>层级</th> <th>全局（个人）</th> <th>代码库（团队）</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>AGENTS</td> <td><code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code></td> <td>代码库根目录或嵌套目录中的 <code>AGENTS.md</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Skills</td> <td><code>~/.agents/skills</code></td> <td>代码库中的 <code>.agents/skills</code></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Codex 会渐进式读取 Skills：</p> <ul> <li>首先读取元数据（<code>name</code> 和 <code>description</code>），以便发现可用的 Skill；</li> <li>选定 Skill 后，再读取完整的 <code>SKILL.md</code>；</li> <li>只有在需要时，才继续读取参考资料或运行脚本。</li> </ul> <p>Skills 可以由用户主动调用；当任务符合 Skill 的适用范围时，Codex 也可以自行选择并使用它。清晰的 Skill 描述有助于 Codex 更准确地判断何时使用它。</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">构建 Skills</a></p> <h2 id=\"mcp\">MCP</h2> <p>MCP（Model Context Protocol，模型上下文协议）是连接 Codex 与外部工具及上下文提供方的标准方式。</p> <p>当 Codex 需要访问本地代码库以外的能力时，MCP 尤其有用，例如任务跟踪器、设计工具、浏览器或共享文档服务。</p> <p>可以这样理解 MCP 中的几个角色：</p> <ul> <li><strong>Host（宿主）：</strong>Codex。</li> <li><strong>Client（客户端）：</strong>Codex 内部的 MCP 连接。</li> <li><strong>Server（服务器）：</strong>外部工具或上下文提供方。</li> </ul> <p>MCP 服务器可以提供：</p> <ul> <li><strong>工具（Tools）：</strong>可执行操作。</li> <li><strong>资源（Resources）：</strong>可读取的数据。</li> <li><strong>提示（Prompts）：</strong>可重复使用的提示模板。</li> </ul> <p>区分这些能力有助于理解信任边界和操作权限。有些服务器主要提供上下文，另一些则会开放可以执行操作的工具。</p> <p>MCP 通常与 Skills 配合使用效果最好：</p> <ul> <li>Skill 负责定义工作流程，并说明要使用哪些 MCP 工具。</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Model Context Protocol</a></p> <p><a id=\"subagents\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">子代理</h2> <p>你可以为不同子代理设定不同角色，并要求它们以不同方式使用工具。例如，一个子代理可以负责运行测试，另一个则可以连接到能读取生产日志的 MCP 服务器。每个子代理都能专注于自己的任务，并使用合适的工具完成工作。</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">子代理</a></p> <p><a id=\"skills-mcp-together\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"skills-mcp\">Skills 与 MCP 配合使用</h2> <p>Skills 与 MCP 配合使用时，工作流程与连接能力就能组合起来：Skills 定义可重复执行的流程，MCP 则连接到外部系统。</p> <p>如果 Skill 依赖 MCP，请在 <code>agents/openai.yaml</code> 中声明该依赖，以便插件自动安装并完成连接配置（参见<a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">构建 Skills</a>）。</p> <p><a id=\"next-step\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">下一步</h2> <p>建议按以下顺序搭建：</p> <ol> <li>先使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">AGENTS.md 提供自定义指令</a>，让 Codex 遵守代码库约定；再添加 pre-commit 钩子和代码检查器来执行这些规则。</li> <li>如果已有可复用的工作流程，就安装相应的<a href=\"#/article/a-dea5d8982f047a3a\">插件</a>。否则，先创建一个 <a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">Skill</a>，需要与团队共享时再将其封装为插件。</li> <li>如果工作流程需要连接外部系统（例如 Linear、GitHub、文档服务或设计工具），再使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a>。</li> <li>熟悉这些基础机制后，如果需要分派繁杂或专业化任务，再使用<a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">子代理</a>。</li> </ol>","en":"<h1 id=\"customization\">Customization</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Customization is how you make Codex work the way your team works.</p> <p>In Codex, customization comes from a few layers that work together:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Project guidance (<code>AGENTS.md</code>)</strong> for persistent instructions</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-7ea5a1f5cc6a25bc\">Memories</a></strong> for useful context learned from prior work</li> <li><strong>Skills</strong> for reusable workflows and domain expertise</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a></strong> for access to external tools and shared systems</li> <li><strong><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">Subagents</a></strong> for delegating work to specialized subagents</li> </ul> <p>These are complementary, not competing. <code>AGENTS.md</code> shapes behavior, memories carry local context forward, skills package repeatable processes, and <a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a> connects Codex to systems outside the local workspace.</p> <h2 id=\"agents-guidance\">AGENTS Guidance</h2> <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> gives Codex durable project guidance that travels with your repository and applies before the agent starts work. Keep it small.</p> <p>Use it for the rules you want Codex to follow every time in a repo, such as:</p> <ul> <li>Build and test commands</li> <li>Review expectations</li> <li>repo-specific conventions</li> <li>Directory-specific instructions</li> </ul> <p>When the agent makes incorrect assumptions about your codebase, correct them in <code>AGENTS.md</code> and ask the agent to update <code>AGENTS.md</code> so the fix persists. Treat it as a feedback loop.</p> <p><strong>Updating <code>AGENTS.md</code>:</strong> Start with only the instructions that matter. Codify recurring review feedback, put guidance in the closest directory where it applies, and tell the agent to update <code>AGENTS.md</code> when you correct something so future sessions inherit the fix.</p> <h3 id=\"when-to-update-agentsmd\">When to update <code>AGENTS.md</code></h3> <ul> <li><strong>Repeated mistakes</strong>: If the agent makes the same mistake repeatedly, add a rule.</li> <li><strong>Too much reading</strong>: If it finds the right files but reads too many documents, add routing guidance (which directories/files to prioritize).</li> <li><strong>Recurring PR feedback</strong>: If you leave the same feedback more than once, codify it.</li> <li><strong>In GitHub</strong>: In a pull request comment, tag <code>@codex</code> with a request (for example, <code>@codex add this to AGENTS.md</code>) to delegate the update to a cloud chat.</li> <li><strong>Automate drift checks</strong>: Use <a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">scheduled tasks</a> to run recurring checks (for example, daily) that look for guidance gaps and suggest what to add to <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Pair <code>AGENTS.md</code> with infrastructure that enforces those rules: pre-commit hooks, linters, and type checkers catch issues before you see them, so the system gets smarter about preventing recurring mistakes.</p> <p>Codex can load guidance from multiple locations: a global file in your Codex home directory (for you as a developer) and repo-specific files that teams can check in. Files closer to the working directory take precedence. Use the global file to shape how Codex communicates with you (for example, review style, verbosity, and defaults), and keep repo files focused on team and codebase rules.</p> <p><strong>File structure</strong></p> <ul> <li><code>~/.codex/</code></li> <li><code>AGENTS.md</code> — Global (for you as a developer)</li> <li><code>repo-root/</code></li> <li><code>AGENTS.md</code> — repo-specific (for your team)</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">Custom instructions with AGENTS.md</a></p> <h2 id=\"skills\">Skills</h2> <p>Skills give Codex reusable capabilities for repeatable workflows. Skills are often the best fit for reusable workflows because they support richer instructions, scripts, and references while staying reusable across tasks. Skills are loaded and visible to the agent (at least their metadata), so Codex can discover and choose them implicitly. This keeps rich workflows available without bloating context up front.</p> <p>Use skill folders to author and iterate on workflows locally. If a plugin already exists for the workflow, install it first to reuse a proven setup. When you want to distribute your own workflow across teams or bundle it with connectors, package it as a <a href=\"#/article/a-0d81218eacd2463c\">plugin</a>. Skills remain the authoring format; plugins are the installable distribution unit.</p> <p>A skill is typically a <code>SKILL.md</code> file plus optional scripts, references, and assets.</p> <p><strong>File structure</strong></p> <ul> <li><code>my-skill/</code></li> <li><code>SKILL.md</code> — Required: instructions + metadata</li> <li><code>scripts/</code> — Optional: executable code</li> <li><code>references/</code> — Optional: documentation</li> <li><code>assets/</code> — Optional: templates, resources</li> </ul> <p>The skill directory can include a <code>scripts/</code> folder with CLI scripts that Codex invokes as part of the workflow (for example, seed data or run validations). When the workflow needs external systems (issue trackers, design tools, docs servers), pair the skill with <a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a>.</p> <p>Example <code>SKILL.md</code>:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-md\">---\nname: commit\ndescription: Stage and commit changes in semantic groups. Use when the user wants to commit, organize commits, or clean up a branch before pushing.\n---\n\n1. Do not run `git add .`. Stage files in logical groups by purpose.\n2. Group into separate commits: feat → test → docs → refactor → chore.\n3. Write concise commit messages that match the change scope.\n4. Keep each commit focused and reviewable.\n</code></pre> <p>Use skills for:</p> <ul> <li>Repeatable workflows (release steps, review routines, docs updates)</li> <li>Team-specific expertise</li> <li>Procedures that need examples, references, or helper scripts</li> </ul> <p>Skills can be global (in your user directory, for you as a developer) or repo-specific (checked into <code>.agents/skills</code>, for your team). Put repo skills in <code>.agents/skills</code> when the workflow applies to that project; use your user directory for skills you want across all repos.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Layer</th> <th>Global</th> <th>repo</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>AGENTS</td> <td><code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code></td> <td><code>AGENTS.md</code> in repo root or nested directories</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Skills</td> <td><code>~/.agents/skills</code></td> <td><code>.agents/skills</code> in repo</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Codex uses progressive disclosure for skills:</p> <ul> <li>It starts with metadata (<code>name</code>, <code>description</code>) for discovery</li> <li>It loads <code>SKILL.md</code> only when a skill is chosen</li> <li>It reads references or runs scripts only when needed</li> </ul> <p>Skills can be invoked explicitly, and Codex can also choose them implicitly when the task matches the skill description. Clear skill descriptions improve triggering reliability.</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">Build skills</a></p> <h2 id=\"mcp\">MCP</h2> <p>MCP (Model Context Protocol) is the standard way to connect Codex to external tools and context providers. It's especially useful for remotely hosted systems such as Figma, Linear, GitHub, or internal knowledge services your team depends on.</p> <p>Use MCP when Codex needs capabilities that live outside the local repo, such as issue trackers, design tools, browsers, or shared documentation systems.</p> <p>One way to think about it:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Host</strong>: Codex</li> <li><strong>Client</strong>: the MCP connection inside Codex</li> <li><strong>Server</strong>: the external tool or context provider</li> </ul> <p>MCP servers can expose:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Tools</strong> (actions)</li> <li><strong>Resources</strong> (readable data)</li> <li><strong>Prompts</strong> (reusable prompt templates)</li> </ul> <p>This separation helps you reason about trust and capability boundaries. Some servers mainly provide context, while others expose powerful actions.</p> <p>In practice, MCP is often most useful when paired with skills:</p> <ul> <li>A skill defines the workflow and names the MCP tools to use</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Model Context Protocol</a></p> <h2 id=\"subagents\">Subagents</h2> <p>You can create different agents with different roles and prompt them to use tools differently. For example, one agent might run specific testing commands and configurations, while another has MCP servers that fetch production logs for debugging. Each subagent stays focused and uses the right tools for its job.</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">Subagents</a></p> <h2 id=\"skills-mcp-together\">Skills + MCP together</h2> <p>Skills plus MCP is where it all comes together: skills define repeatable workflows, and MCP connects them to external tools and systems. If a skill depends on MCP, declare that dependency in <code>agents/openai.yaml</code> so Codex can install and wire it automatically (see <a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">Build skills</a>).</p> <h2 id=\"next-step\">Next step</h2> <p>Build in this order:</p> <ol> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">Custom instructions with AGENTS.md</a> so Codex follows your repo conventions. Add pre-commit hooks and linters to enforce those rules.</li> <li>Install a <a href=\"#/article/a-dea5d8982f047a3a\">plugin</a> when a reusable workflow already exists. Otherwise, create a <a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">skill</a> and package it as a plugin when you want to share it.</li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP</a> when workflows need external systems (Linear, GitHub, docs servers, design tools).</li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">Subagents</a> when you're ready to delegate noisy or specialized tasks to subagents.</li> </ol>"};
